<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Kalijar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1009">1009</a>–<a href="/years/1048">1048</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Kalijar was a Buyid ruler who governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzistan</a> from 1024 and also <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> from 1044 until his death in 1048. One of the last capable members of the dynasty, he reunited much of its southern territory, but his reign was troubled by quarrels with his relatives and by the growing threat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a>, whom his weaker successors could not resist.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-kalijar.jpg" alt="Abu Kalijar" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group; [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AbuKalijarBuyidCoinHistoryofIran.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samsam-al-dawla">Abu Kalijar Marzuban</a> was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> al-Dawla. His father's death in 1024 set off a succession crisis within the Buyid state, and not until 1027 did the army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> choose Sultan al-Dawla's brother Jalal al-Dawla as ruler. In the meantime Abu Kalijar built up his power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>, although in the first years of his rule he stood under the oversight of his tutor, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a> named Sandal, and was drawn into a conflict with the Buyid ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a>, Qawam al-Dawla.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The death of Qawam al-Dawla in 1028 allowed Abu Kalijar to occupy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a>. In 1030 Jalal al-Dawla sent a fleet of 1300 ships under his vizier Abu Ali Hasan to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> from him, but the expedition ended in complete defeat and the vizier was briefly held prisoner. Abu Kalijar made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahvaz">Ahvaz</a> his capital. When the Ghaznavids invaded Kerman in 1033, the burdens they laid on its people turned them back towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a>, and in the following year his vizier Bahram ibn Mafinna drove the Ghaznavids out.</p><p>Around 1037 his army marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. It did not take the city, but Jalal al-Dawla recognised him as senior amir, and Abu Kalijar began to use the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahanshah">Shahanshah</a> on his coins. Because the amir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> and the Arab tribe of the Asadids backed Jalal al-Dawla, the two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> came to a compromise, each using the same titles and ruling independently; Abu Kalijar did make his son governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>.</p><p>After Bahram ibn Mafinna died in 1041/1042, Dhu'l-Sa'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adat">adat</a> became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>. The death of Jalal al-Dawla in 1044 gave Abu Kalijar Iraq, but his hold on it stayed weak and he kept his capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahvaz">Ahvaz</a>. In the same year he strengthened the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> and tried to force the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kakuyids">Kakuyids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, then divided between two rival brothers, to submit, but they recognised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> as their overlords instead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Abu Kalijar later travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, where he received the title of senior amir and the title Muhyi al-Din, Reviver of Religion. Several minor rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> recognised his authority, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kakuyids">Kakuyids</a> too declared their allegiance. This brought a Seljuk intervention, and Abu Kalijar chose to negotiate and arrange a marriage alliance. When the Buyid governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> submitted to the Seljuk Qavurt, Abu Kalijar marched against him, but was met by an envoy bearing gifts and a promise of renewed loyalty. He died soon afterwards, in October 1048, at the age of thirty-eight. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-malik-al-rahim">al-Malik al-Rahim</a> succeeded him, but a succession struggle followed and Kerman passed into the Seljuk orbit.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abu Kalijar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Abu Kalijar</b> held Fars and Iraq until 1048, but his successors were too weak to resist the Seljuks.</blockquote><a href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahvaz">Ahvaz</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.52,47.81&amp;year=1034" aria-label="Basra on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Abu Kalijar"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4109 4908h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4109 4908h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=30.52,47.81&amp;year=1034">What was here in 1034? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
